'''''Allamanda blanchetii''''' ('''purple allamanda''', '''violet allamanda'''; Pilaghanti in Sanskrit [[synonym (taxonomy)|syn.]] ''Allamanda violacea'') is a species of perennial flowering plant in the family [[Apocynaceae]] native to [[Brazil]].<ref>{{GRIN | accessdate = 21 January 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Plants Profile for Allamanda blanchetii (purple allamanda)|url=http://plants.usda.gov/plant-profile?symbol=ALBL|access-date=2020-07-16|website=plants.usda.gov}}</ref> Cultivated as an [[ornamental plant]], it grows in full sun in USDA Zones 9b through 11<ref>{{cite web|last=Gilman|first=Edward|orig-date=1999|edition=Revised|year=2018|title=Allamanda violacea Purple Allamanda|url=https://edis.ifas.ufl.edu/fp032|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080318134728/http://edis.ifas.ufl.edu/FP032|archive-date=2008-03-18|access-date=July 17, 2020|website=University of Florida Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences}}</ref> including central and south Florida, southernmost Texas, and coastal California. This plant's purple, bell-shaped blooms are about 3" across.<ref>{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=Allamanda, Golden Trumpet, PURPLE ALLAMANDA|url=https://www.southernliving.com/plants/allamanda|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200717130026/https://www.southernliving.com/plants/allamanda |archive-date=2020-07-17 |access-date=July 16, 2020|website=Southern Living}}</ref> It is also called red bell. From the stem, fiber can be extracted which is very strong and silky white after chemical treatment.<ref>{{Cite journal|date=25 December 2021|title=Fiber|journal=Carbohydrate Polymer Technologies and Applications|volume=2|doi=10.1016/j.carpta.2021.100069|last1=Subramanian|first1=Kaliappa|last2=Vijayakumar|first2=Vediappan|doi-access=free}}</ref>

